{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-71892",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134723/assets/726ad8599b89029d3dba8e79ca947af99c17bd8712e551e49fc33d3e597a5cb7.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-3\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-y}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-y}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
